<article class="env-remark" id="0201">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0201">Remark <span data-tag="0201">30.18.2</span>.</a> In the situation of Chow's Lemma <a href="/tag/0200" data-tag="0200">30.18.1</a>: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The morphism $\pi $ is actually H-projective (hence projective, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01W9" data-tag="01W9">29.44.3</a>) since the morphism $X' \to \mathbf{P}^ n_ S \times _ S X = \mathbf{P}^ n_ X$ is a closed immersion (use the fact that $\pi $ is proper, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01W6" data-tag="01W6">29.42.7</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We may assume that $\pi ^{-1}(U)$ is scheme theoretically dense in $X'$. Namely, we can simply replace $X'$ by the scheme theoretic closure of $\pi ^{-1}(U)$. In this case we can think of $U$ as a scheme theoretically dense open subscheme of $X'$. See Morphisms, Section <a href="/tag/01R5" data-tag="01R5">29.6</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $X$ is reduced then we may choose $X'$ reduced. This is clear from (2). 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>